abstract = {This paper proposes a Multi-Agent Reinforcement Learning~(MARL)-based supervisory controller for post-fault voltage recovery in Active Distribution Networks~(ADNs) with photovoltaic~(PV) inverter resources. The controller coordinates inverter reactive power setpoints after fault clearing, while its actions are held fixed during the fault-on interval to avoid interference with fast inverter inner-loop and fault ride-through dynamics. The approach is evaluated using RMS dynamic simulations on a modified IEEE 33-bus distribution network over 101 operating and fault scenarios. Compared with droop and fixed-parameter PI baselines, the MARL policies mainly improve transient recovery behavior, particularly in terms of settling time, post-fault time within the admissible voltage band, and voltage violation area. The fixed-PI controller provides the best final voltage accuracy and shortest first recovery time, but exhibits longer settling behavior. Among the learned policies, Fast MARL achieves the strongest recovery-oriented performance, whereas Balanced MARL provides a more conservative response with lower voltage peaks and reduced peak reactive power injection. The results indicate that MARL-based supervisory coordination can provide flexible trade-offs between recovery quality and control effort.}
}
